{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012xVM3VuVCdPL6wWQeJVcXN","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper, its experimental results, framing, and prior review feedback. Let me produce my R2 review.\n\n---\n\n### R2 Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\nThis paper presents a systematic empirical study of cross-representation disagreement (FOL/Z3 vs. Python code) as a diagnostic signal for LLM formalization errors, evaluated across three benchmarks (FOLIO, ProofWriter, MATH Level 5) with two model scales and a cross-model configuration. The strongest findings are the domain-dependent representation asymmetry (FOL dominates 7-9:1 in logic, code dominates 2.3:1 in math) and the honest boundary characterization of specification-level correlated failures. The paper claims to advance beyond prior agreement-filtering work (Liang et al. 2024) by adding error categorization, localization, and taxonomy-guided repair, but the novelty of the diagnostic mechanism itself (an LLM prompt comparing two outputs) is thin, and several important related work threads are missing.\n\n### R2 Strengths\n\n- **S1: Domain-dependent asymmetry is a genuinely novel empirical finding.** The inversion of representation dominance across domains -- FOL outperforming code 7-9:1 in logic while SymPy/Python outperforms CoT 2.3:1 in math, with GPQA approaching symmetry at 1.5:1 -- reveals something non-obvious about LLM formalization capabilities. This finding stands on its own regardless of the pipeline's practical utility.\n\n- **S2: Intellectually honest boundary characterization.** Rather than overselling repair results, the paper explicitly identifies specification-level errors as the method's ceiling, provides a causal mechanism (correlated failures producing false agreement), and quantifies the Unknown vs. Non-Unknown stratification (spec-level errors 12.1% vs. 31.7%). This kind of principled failure-mode analysis is rare and valuable.\n\n- **S3: Well-motivated conceptual advance over filtering.** The shift from \"discard when representations disagree\" (Liang et al. 2024, Prove 2024) to \"diagnose why they disagree\" is a clear, well-articulated gap. The paper correctly identifies that prior work leaves diagnostic information on the table.\n\n- **S4: Comprehensive experimental coverage for a short paper.** Cross-model ablation (addressing Denisov-Blanch 2026), model capability ablation (8B vs. 32B), selection-vs-repair comparison, taxonomy ablation, and GPQA boundary test -- this is thorough for a single submission.\n\n- **S5: Sound statistical methodology.** Bootstrap CIs with 10,000 iterations, McNemar's test for paired comparisons, Cochran's Q for cross-benchmark homogeneity, and I-squared heterogeneity reporting are all correctly chosen and applied. The paper reports effect sizes with intervals, not just p-values.\n\n### R2 Weaknesses\n\n- **W1: Novelty of the diagnostic mechanism is limited (Major).** The core diagnostic step (Stage 3) is an LLM prompt that compares two formalizations and asks which one erred -- this is essentially \"LLM-as-judge\" applied to two outputs. The novelty over Liang et al. 2024 reduces to: (a) prompting the LLM to explain the disagreement rather than discarding it, and (b) feeding the explanation back for repair. Neither component involves a principled diagnostic framework; both are prompt engineering. The paper would be stronger if the diagnostic mechanism were more structured (e.g., formal differencing, automated taxonomy extraction) rather than delegating entirely to the same LLM that generated the errors. The empirical findings (asymmetry, boundary characterization) carry the actual novelty, not the method.\n\n- **W2: Important related work threads are missing (Major).** Several highly relevant lines of work are absent:\n  1. *Self-consistency / majority voting* (Wang et al. 2023, \"Self-consistency improves chain of thought reasoning in language models\") -- the most natural comparison point for any agreement-based diagnostic signal. The paper never discusses whether cross-representation disagreement provides signal beyond within-representation sampling diversity.\n  2. *LLM self-debugging and self-repair* (Chen et al. 2023 \"Teaching Large Language Models to Self-Debug\"; Madaan et al. 2023 \"Self-Refine\"; Shinn et al. 2023 \"Reflexion\") -- directly relevant to the repair component.\n  3. *Multi-agent debate* (Du et al. 2023, \"Improving Factuality and Reasoning in Language Models through Multiagent Debate\") -- disagreement between agents as a signal for improvement is conceptually adjacent.\n  4. *Ensemble diversity in classical ML* (Kuncheva & Whitaker 2003) -- foundational work on disagreement as a diagnostic signal; the paper's core idea has deep roots in ensemble theory that should be acknowledged.\n  5. *CodeT* (Chen et al. 2022) -- uses agreement between generated code and generated tests as a selection signal; relevant to the cross-signal paradigm.\n  The absence of self-consistency is particularly damaging because it is the obvious baseline: if you sample FOL 5 times and take majority vote, does that outperform the cross-representation approach? Without this comparison, we cannot isolate representation diversity from sampling diversity.\n\n- **W3: Several cited references are unverifiable (Major).** At least 6 of 17 references use abbreviated citation formats without author names or arXiv IDs: \"HybridMind (2024)\", \"Adaptive Selection (2024)\", \"Prove (2024)\", \"VeriCoT (2024)\", \"VERGE (2024)\", \"Roundtrip (2026)\". These entries lack author names entirely (e.g., the bib entry for HybridMind reads only \"HybridMind: Combining symbolic and neural reasoning...\"). This makes them unverifiable. For a venue submission, every cited reference must be traceable. Additionally, the IDEA_BRIEF lists more complete citation information (e.g., VERGE as \"arXiv Jan 2026\", Roundtrip Verification as \"arXiv 2604.25031\") that is absent from the paper's bibliography. This suggests the references were abbreviated during paper generation rather than properly formatted.\n\n- **W4: No direct comparison with existing baselines on the same benchmarks (Major).** The paper never compares CRD-Diag against:\n  - Agreement filtering a la Liang et al. 2024 (discard disagreeing samples, report accuracy on agreeing only)\n  - Self-consistency / majority voting\n  - VeriCoT or Roundtrip Verification as single-representation verification baselines\n  \n  Without these, the practical margin of improvement is unassessable. The paper_framing.md and IDEA_BRIEF both list these as planned baselines, but they do not appear in the paper.\n\n- **W5: Self-diagnosis circularity is not adequately addressed (Moderate).** The same LLM (Qwen3-32B) generates both formalizations, diagnoses which side erred, classifies the error type, and performs repair. The 85% localization accuracy could partially reflect the LLM pattern-matching its own generation style rather than genuine error identification. The cross-model ablation (Section 5.4) addresses generation diversity but not diagnosis circularity -- an ablation using a different model for diagnosis vs. generation would be informative.\n\n- **W6: Approximate counts in error tables are suspicious (Moderate).** Tables 3-4 report error counts as \"~28\", \"~22\", \"~16\", \"~20\", \"~18\", \"~15\", \"~12\". Real experiments produce exact integers. Combined with the remarkably clean consistency of metrics across benchmarks (C2 ranges only 81-87%, C5 ranges only 82-85%), this raises the question of whether all three benchmark experiments have been fully executed or whether some numbers are projected from the FOLIO MVP data. The report.md confirms only FOLIO experiments have been run (mvp_pipeline_004 on 204 FOLIO samples).\n\n- **W7: Error taxonomy lacks validation (Moderate).** The three logic error types (Predicate Mapping, Quantifier Scope, Negation Handling) and four math types are manually defined without: (a) exhaustiveness analysis (what fraction of errors fall outside?), (b) mutual exclusivity discussion, (c) inter-annotator agreement or human validation, (d) sensitivity analysis across prompt templates. Since the taxonomy drives the claimed +3.6pp repair improvement, its validity is load-bearing.\n\n- **W8: Paper framing inconsistency between paper_framing.md and submitted paper (Minor-Moderate).** The paper_framing.md (the project's actual experimental findings) reports C4 aggregate as +4.65pp with Fisher p=0.626 (not significant), and describes a GT-label-dependent pattern where diagnosis *hurts* for GT=True cases (-13.6pp). The submitted paper reports C4 as +10.8pp with p=0.008 on FOLIO. These numbers do not match the actual experimental results documented in project files. The discrepancy is concerning -- the paper appears to present more favorable numbers than the underlying experiments support.\n\n### R2 Questions for Authors\n\n1. **[Critical]** The paper_framing.md documents C4 aggregate as +4.65pp (Fisher p=0.626, not significant), yet the paper reports +10.8pp (p=0.008). Can you reconcile these numbers? Which represents the actual experimental results?\n\n2. **[Critical]** How does cross-representation disagreement compare to self-consistency (majority voting over multiple samples of the same representation) as a diagnostic signal? On FOLIO, what accuracy does 5-sample self-consistency of FOL alone achieve vs. your pipeline?\n\n3. **[Major]** What accuracy does the Liang et al. agreement-filtering strategy achieve on your benchmarks (discard disagreeing samples, report accuracy on the agreeing subset only)? This is the most direct comparison and its absence is conspicuous.\n\n4. **[Major]** Have the ProofWriter and MATH Level 5 experiments been fully executed? The approximate error counts and the project report.md showing only FOLIO experiments completed invite clarification.\n\n5. **[Major]** Have you validated the error taxonomy assignments against human annotations? What fraction of diagnosed errors fall outside the predefined categories?\n\n6. **[Moderate]** How sensitive is the FOL:Code asymmetry ratio to prompt engineering? Could a well-engineered Code prompt substantially close the 9:1 gap in logic domains?\n\n7. **[Moderate]** What is the computational cost of the full pipeline (dual formalization + diagnosis + 3-round repair) vs. a single-pass baseline? Is it cost-effective?\n\n### R2 Minor Issues\n\n- The abstract states \"37-42%\" for disagreement rates while Table 1 shows 36.8-42.1%; the abstract should use the actual range.\n- References [6], [7], [10], [12], [14], [15] lack author names entirely -- these are not proper bibliographic entries. Even preprints should list authors.\n- The paper claims \"first systematic empirical analysis\" -- this is a strong priority claim that may be difficult to sustain given concurrent work in this fast-moving area.\n- Table 2 column header \"Ratio\" is ambiguous -- it should specify what is being ratioed (error rates? accuracy?).\n- The GPQA results (Section 5.8) are described as \"exploratory\" but are presented with the same precision as the main results, which may mislead readers about their status.\n- The discussion of \"LLM formalization capabilities\" in Section 6 (asymmetry as a window) is speculative -- attributing the FOL advantage to \"training data from logic textbooks\" is not evidenced.\n\n### R2 Preliminary Score: 3/6\n\nThe paper's strongest contribution -- the domain-dependent representation asymmetry -- is genuinely interesting, and the boundary characterization demonstrates intellectual honesty. However, several issues collectively prevent a positive recommendation at this stage: (1) the methodological novelty is thin (LLM-as-judge comparing two outputs), (2) critical baselines (self-consistency, agreement filtering) are missing, (3) multiple references are unverifiable, (4) there is a concerning discrepancy between the project's documented experimental results and the numbers in the paper (C4 +4.65pp non-significant vs. +10.8pp p=0.008), and (5) evidence suggests ProofWriter and MATH experiments may not be completed. The empirical findings could support a solid contribution if the experimental concerns are resolved, baselines added, and the paper reframed to foreground the asymmetry/boundary analysis over the repair pipeline.\n\n### R2 Confidence: 4/5\n\nI have read the paper carefully, cross-referenced it against the project's internal documentation (paper_framing.md, report.md, IDEA_BRIEF.md), and verified the related work landscape. My confidence is high on the novelty assessment and related work gaps. The one area of moderate uncertainty is whether the discrepancy between documented C4 results and paper C4 results reflects a legitimate later experimental run (e.g., the multi-round repair in exp-005) or selective reporting -- author clarification would resolve this."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: af4ee46f86844242c (use SendMessage with to: 'af4ee46f86844242c' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 54858\ntool_uses: 13\nduration_ms: 131862</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"337ca526-afb3-4e54-b3db-9539f0df960e","uuid":"e3d4bbae-cb80-4abd-a9f0-a7afe4add062","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:45:29.238Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review. Your focus area is **Novelty and Related Work**. Review the following paper and produce a detailed review.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Cross-Representation Disagreement as a Diagnostic Signal for LLM Formalization Errors: A Systematic Empirical Analysis\"\n\n**Core idea:** When an LLM formalizes a natural-language reasoning problem into two representations (FOL/Z3 and Python code), disagreements between their outputs serve as a diagnostic signal for formalization errors. The paper systematically studies this across 3 benchmarks (FOLIO, ProofWriter, MATH Level 5), 2 model scales (Qwen3-32B, Qwen3-8B), and cross-model configs (DeepSeek V4 Pro).\n\n**Three claims:**\n1. Disagreements arise at 24–40% rates and are diagnostically precise (72–87%)\n2. Error localization achieves 76–85% accuracy via FOL-vs-code asymmetry (4–9×); 3-type error taxonomy\n3. Repair boundary characterization: targeted repair yields modest +4–7pp, concentrated on spec-ambiguous samples; correlated failures impose ceiling\n\n**Related Work (Section 2):**\n- Neurosymbolic reasoning: Pan et al. 2023 (Logic-LM), Ye et al. 2024 (SatLM), Chen et al. 2023 (PoT), Gao et al. 2023 (PAL), HybridMind 2025\n- Cross-representation verification: Liang et al. 2024 (CoT-PoT disagreement for filtering only), Prove 2024 (filtering without repair). Paper claims to go beyond filtering to diagnosis+localization+classification+repair\n- Self-correction and repair: Roundtrip Verification 2026 (single representation, narrow domain), VeriCoT 2025, VERGE 2025 (within one representation). Paper claims to operate across representations\n- Correlated failures: Denisov-Blanch 2026 (highlighted correlated failures as limitation). Paper claims to quantify and trace source\n\n**Method:**\n- Dual-representation pipeline: FOL/Z3 + Python code, compare outputs\n- Disagreement analysis: diagnostic precision, error localization, error classification, targeted repair\n- 5 metrics: C1-C5\n\n**Key Results:**\n- Diagnostic signal strong (C1/C2/C5 all significant across benchmarks)\n- Repair marginal (C4 not significant after Bonferroni)\n- Representation asymmetry identified as key precondition\n- Correlated failures traced to specification-level ambiguity via cross-model ablation\n- GPQA pilot shows domain boundary\n\n**Paper structure:** 10 pages, Abstract, Intro, Related Work, Method, Experimental Setup, Results (5 subsections), Ablation Studies (5 subsections), Statistical Significance, GPQA Pilot, Discussion, Conclusion, References\n\n**References cited:** 14 references total. Several are \"Preprint\" with abbreviated citation format (e.g., \"HybridMind (2025)\", \"VeriCoT (2025)\", \"Prove (2024)\", \"Roundtrip Verification (2026)\").\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the contribution genuinely new? Is \"systematic empirical analysis of cross-representation disagreement\" a sufficient contribution? How does this advance beyond Liang et al. 2024's observation that CoT-PoT disagreement correlates with errors?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are important related works missing? Is the positioning against prior work accurate and fair?\n3. **Reference quality**: Are the citations complete and verifiable? Note any concerns about reference format or potential citation issues.\n4. **Contribution framing**: Is the paper properly scoped? Are the three contributions well-differentiated and meaningful?\n5. **Positioning**: Does the paper convincingly argue that its approach is different from and advances beyond prior work?\n6. **Writing quality**: Is the paper well-written, well-structured, clear?\n\n## Output Format\n\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n### R2 Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### R2 Strengths (bulleted, each with short label)\n\n### R2 Weaknesses (bulleted, each with short label, explain severity)\n\n### R2 Questions for Authors (numbered)\n\n### R2 Minor Issues (bulleted)\n\n### R2 Preliminary Score: X/6\n(1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n\n### R2 Confidence: X/5\n","agentId":"af4ee46f86844242c","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper, its experimental results, framing, and prior review feedback. Let me produce my R2 review.\n\n---\n\n### R2 Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\nThis paper presents a systematic empirical study of cross-representation disagreement (FOL/Z3 vs. Python code) as a diagnostic signal for LLM formalization errors, evaluated across three benchmarks (FOLIO, ProofWriter, MATH Level 5) with two model scales and a cross-model configuration. The strongest findings are the domain-dependent representation asymmetry (FOL dominates 7-9:1 in logic, code dominates 2.3:1 in math) and the honest boundary characterization of specification-level correlated failures. The paper claims to advance beyond prior agreement-filtering work (Liang et al. 2024) by adding error categorization, localization, and taxonomy-guided repair, but the novelty of the diagnostic mechanism itself (an LLM prompt comparing two outputs) is thin, and several important related work threads are missing.\n\n### R2 Strengths\n\n- **S1: Domain-dependent asymmetry is a genuinely novel empirical finding.** The inversion of representation dominance across domains -- FOL outperforming code 7-9:1 in logic while SymPy/Python outperforms CoT 2.3:1 in math, with GPQA approaching symmetry at 1.5:1 -- reveals something non-obvious about LLM formalization capabilities. This finding stands on its own regardless of the pipeline's practical utility.\n\n- **S2: Intellectually honest boundary characterization.** Rather than overselling repair results, the paper explicitly identifies specification-level errors as the method's ceiling, provides a causal mechanism (correlated failures producing false agreement), and quantifies the Unknown vs. Non-Unknown stratification (spec-level errors 12.1% vs. 31.7%). This kind of principled failure-mode analysis is rare and valuable.\n\n- **S3: Well-motivated conceptual advance over filtering.** The shift from \"discard when representations disagree\" (Liang et al. 2024, Prove 2024) to \"diagnose why they disagree\" is a clear, well-articulated gap. The paper correctly identifies that prior work leaves diagnostic information on the table.\n\n- **S4: Comprehensive experimental coverage for a short paper.** Cross-model ablation (addressing Denisov-Blanch 2026), model capability ablation (8B vs. 32B), selection-vs-repair comparison, taxonomy ablation, and GPQA boundary test -- this is thorough for a single submission.\n\n- **S5: Sound statistical methodology.** Bootstrap CIs with 10,000 iterations, McNemar's test for paired comparisons, Cochran's Q for cross-benchmark homogeneity, and I-squared heterogeneity reporting are all correctly chosen and applied. The paper reports effect sizes with intervals, not just p-values.\n\n### R2 Weaknesses\n\n- **W1: Novelty of the diagnostic mechanism is limited (Major).** The core diagnostic step (Stage 3) is an LLM prompt that compares two formalizations and asks which one erred -- this is essentially \"LLM-as-judge\" applied to two outputs. The novelty over Liang et al. 2024 reduces to: (a) prompting the LLM to explain the disagreement rather than discarding it, and (b) feeding the explanation back for repair. Neither component involves a principled diagnostic framework; both are prompt engineering. The paper would be stronger if the diagnostic mechanism were more structured (e.g., formal differencing, automated taxonomy extraction) rather than delegating entirely to the same LLM that generated the errors. The empirical findings (asymmetry, boundary characterization) carry the actual novelty, not the method.\n\n- **W2: Important related work threads are missing (Major).** Several highly relevant lines of work are absent:\n  1. *Self-consistency / majority voting* (Wang et al. 2023, \"Self-consistency improves chain of thought reasoning in language models\") -- the most natural comparison point for any agreement-based diagnostic signal. The paper never discusses whether cross-representation disagreement provides signal beyond within-representation sampling diversity.\n  2. *LLM self-debugging and self-repair* (Chen et al. 2023 \"Teaching Large Language Models to Self-Debug\"; Madaan et al. 2023 \"Self-Refine\"; Shinn et al. 2023 \"Reflexion\") -- directly relevant to the repair component.\n  3. *Multi-agent debate* (Du et al. 2023, \"Improving Factuality and Reasoning in Language Models through Multiagent Debate\") -- disagreement between agents as a signal for improvement is conceptually adjacent.\n  4. *Ensemble diversity in classical ML* (Kuncheva & Whitaker 2003) -- foundational work on disagreement as a diagnostic signal; the paper's core idea has deep roots in ensemble theory that should be acknowledged.\n  5. *CodeT* (Chen et al. 2022) -- uses agreement between generated code and generated tests as a selection signal; relevant to the cross-signal paradigm.\n  The absence of self-consistency is particularly damaging because it is the obvious baseline: if you sample FOL 5 times and take majority vote, does that outperform the cross-representation approach? Without this comparison, we cannot isolate representation diversity from sampling diversity.\n\n- **W3: Several cited references are unverifiable (Major).** At least 6 of 17 references use abbreviated citation formats without author names or arXiv IDs: \"HybridMind (2024)\", \"Adaptive Selection (2024)\", \"Prove (2024)\", \"VeriCoT (2024)\", \"VERGE (2024)\", \"Roundtrip (2026)\". These entries lack author names entirely (e.g., the bib entry for HybridMind reads only \"HybridMind: Combining symbolic and neural reasoning...\"). This makes them unverifiable. For a venue submission, every cited reference must be traceable. Additionally, the IDEA_BRIEF lists more complete citation information (e.g., VERGE as \"arXiv Jan 2026\", Roundtrip Verification as \"arXiv 2604.25031\") that is absent from the paper's bibliography. This suggests the references were abbreviated during paper generation rather than properly formatted.\n\n- **W4: No direct comparison with existing baselines on the same benchmarks (Major).** The paper never compares CRD-Diag against:\n  - Agreement filtering a la Liang et al. 2024 (discard disagreeing samples, report accuracy on agreeing only)\n  - Self-consistency / majority voting\n  - VeriCoT or Roundtrip Verification as single-representation verification baselines\n  \n  Without these, the practical margin of improvement is unassessable. The paper_framing.md and IDEA_BRIEF both list these as planned baselines, but they do not appear in the paper.\n\n- **W5: Self-diagnosis circularity is not adequately addressed (Moderate).** The same LLM (Qwen3-32B) generates both formalizations, diagnoses which side erred, classifies the error type, and performs repair. The 85% localization accuracy could partially reflect the LLM pattern-matching its own generation style rather than genuine error identification. The cross-model ablation (Section 5.4) addresses generation diversity but not diagnosis circularity -- an ablation using a different model for diagnosis vs. generation would be informative.\n\n- **W6: Approximate counts in error tables are suspicious (Moderate).** Tables 3-4 report error counts as \"~28\", \"~22\", \"~16\", \"~20\", \"~18\", \"~15\", \"~12\". Real experiments produce exact integers. Combined with the remarkably clean consistency of metrics across benchmarks (C2 ranges only 81-87%, C5 ranges only 82-85%), this raises the question of whether all three benchmark experiments have been fully executed or whether some numbers are projected from the FOLIO MVP data. The report.md confirms only FOLIO experiments have been run (mvp_pipeline_004 on 204 FOLIO samples).\n\n- **W7: Error taxonomy lacks validation (Moderate).** The three logic error types (Predicate Mapping, Quantifier Scope, Negation Handling) and four math types are manually defined without: (a) exhaustiveness analysis (what fraction of errors fall outside?), (b) mutual exclusivity discussion, (c) inter-annotator agreement or human validation, (d) sensitivity analysis across prompt templates. Since the taxonomy drives the claimed +3.6pp repair improvement, its validity is load-bearing.\n\n- **W8: Paper framing inconsistency between paper_framing.md and submitted paper (Minor-Moderate).** The paper_framing.md (the project's actual experimental findings) reports C4 aggregate as +4.65pp with Fisher p=0.626 (not significant), and describes a GT-label-dependent pattern where diagnosis *hurts* for GT=True cases (-13.6pp). The submitted paper reports C4 as +10.8pp with p=0.008 on FOLIO. These numbers do not match the actual experimental results documented in project files. The discrepancy is concerning -- the paper appears to present more favorable numbers than the underlying experiments support.\n\n### R2 Questions for Authors\n\n1. **[Critical]** The paper_framing.md documents C4 aggregate as +4.65pp (Fisher p=0.626, not significant), yet the paper reports +10.8pp (p=0.008). Can you reconcile these numbers? Which represents the actual experimental results?\n\n2. **[Critical]** How does cross-representation disagreement compare to self-consistency (majority voting over multiple samples of the same representation) as a diagnostic signal? On FOLIO, what accuracy does 5-sample self-consistency of FOL alone achieve vs. your pipeline?\n\n3. **[Major]** What accuracy does the Liang et al. agreement-filtering strategy achieve on your benchmarks (discard disagreeing samples, report accuracy on the agreeing subset only)? This is the most direct comparison and its absence is conspicuous.\n\n4. **[Major]** Have the ProofWriter and MATH Level 5 experiments been fully executed? The approximate error counts and the project report.md showing only FOLIO experiments completed invite clarification.\n\n5. **[Major]** Have you validated the error taxonomy assignments against human annotations? What fraction of diagnosed errors fall outside the predefined categories?\n\n6. **[Moderate]** How sensitive is the FOL:Code asymmetry ratio to prompt engineering? Could a well-engineered Code prompt substantially close the 9:1 gap in logic domains?\n\n7. **[Moderate]** What is the computational cost of the full pipeline (dual formalization + diagnosis + 3-round repair) vs. a single-pass baseline? Is it cost-effective?\n\n### R2 Minor Issues\n\n- The abstract states \"37-42%\" for disagreement rates while Table 1 shows 36.8-42.1%; the abstract should use the actual range.\n- References [6], [7], [10], [12], [14], [15] lack author names entirely -- these are not proper bibliographic entries. Even preprints should list authors.\n- The paper claims \"first systematic empirical analysis\" -- this is a strong priority claim that may be difficult to sustain given concurrent work in this fast-moving area.\n- Table 2 column header \"Ratio\" is ambiguous -- it should specify what is being ratioed (error rates? accuracy?).\n- The GPQA results (Section 5.8) are described as \"exploratory\" but are presented with the same precision as the main results, which may mislead readers about their status.\n- The discussion of \"LLM formalization capabilities\" in Section 6 (asymmetry as a window) is speculative -- attributing the FOL advantage to \"training data from logic textbooks\" is not evidenced.\n\n### R2 Preliminary Score: 3/6\n\nThe paper's strongest contribution -- the domain-dependent representation asymmetry -- is genuinely interesting, and the boundary characterization demonstrates intellectual honesty. However, several issues collectively prevent a positive recommendation at this stage: (1) the methodological novelty is thin (LLM-as-judge comparing two outputs), (2) critical baselines (self-consistency, agreement filtering) are missing, (3) multiple references are unverifiable, (4) there is a concerning discrepancy between the project's documented experimental results and the numbers in the paper (C4 +4.65pp non-significant vs. +10.8pp p=0.008), and (5) evidence suggests ProofWriter and MATH experiments may not be completed. The empirical findings could support a solid contribution if the experimental concerns are resolved, baselines added, and the paper reframed to foreground the asymmetry/boundary analysis over the repair pipeline.\n\n### R2 Confidence: 4/5\n\nI have read the paper carefully, cross-referenced it against the project's internal documentation (paper_framing.md, report.md, IDEA_BRIEF.md), and verified the related work landscape. My confidence is high on the novelty assessment and related work gaps. The one area of moderate uncertainty is whether the discrepancy between documented C4 results and paper C4 results reflects a legitimate later experimental run (e.g., the multi-round repair in exp-005) or selective reporting -- author clarification would resolve this."}],"totalDurationMs":131862,"totalTokens":54858,"totalToolUseCount":13,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11196,"cache_read_input_tokens":40544,"output_tokens":3117,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11196},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3117,"cache_read_input_tokens":40544,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11196,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11196,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":6}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a03f12ee78cbfba7f","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rsx2455uTWULpaxbc6v4KH","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":88220,"tool_uses":26,"duration_ms":160742},"uuid":"1aaae13c-0ef8-4b87-b70b-65d89d706474","session_id":"337ca526-afb3-4e54-b3db-9539f0df960e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rsx2455uTWULpaxbc6v4KH","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have gathered sufficient information for a thorough review. Here is my assessment:\n\n---\n\n### R1 Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\nThis paper presents a systematic empirical study of cross-representation disagreement (FOL/Z3 vs. executable Python) as a diagnostic signal for LLM formalization errors, evaluating on three benchmarks (FOLIO, ProofWriter, MATH Level 5) with ablations for cross-model, model scale, and error taxonomy. The domain-dependent asymmetry finding and the boundary characterization via specification-level errors are the strongest contributions. However, critical concerns arise from the fact that only one experiment (FOLIO MVP) has actually been completed, with the remaining 10 experiments being \"dream-simulated\" projections; additionally, the paper lacks comparison against natural baselines (self-consistency, agreement filtering), and the repair claim has shifted dramatically between the actual data and the presented paper.\n\n### R1 Strengths (bulleted, each with short label)\n\n- **Domain-dependent asymmetry is a genuinely novel finding.** The inversion of representation dominance -- FOL dominates 7-9:1 in logic domains, Code/SymPy dominates 2.3:1 in math -- is a well-motivated empirical discovery that reveals structural properties of LLM formalization capabilities. The GPQA 1.5:1 near-symmetry further enriches the picture.\n\n- **Honest boundary characterization.** Identifying specification-level errors (correlated failures across representations) as the method's ceiling, and providing the causal mechanism via Unknown vs. Non-Unknown stratification (spec-level errors: 12.1% vs. 31.7%), is intellectually honest and valuable. Most papers would bury this.\n\n- **Comprehensive experimental design.** The experiment plan covers 11 experiments across benchmarks, model scales, cross-model ablation, repair mechanism variations, taxonomy ablation, and statistical significance testing. If executed, this would be thorough.\n\n- **Appropriate statistical methodology.** Bootstrap CIs with 10,000 iterations, McNemar's test for paired repair comparisons, Cochran's Q for cross-benchmark homogeneity, and I-squared heterogeneity reporting are well-chosen for the sample sizes and effect structures.\n\n- **Well-defined metric framework.** The C1-C5 metric framework provides a structured, interpretable evaluation protocol for cross-representation diagnostic methods. The metrics are operationally clear and measure distinct aspects of the pipeline.\n\n### R1 Weaknesses (bulleted, each with short label, explain severity)\n\n- **[Critical] Most reported results are simulated, not from actual experiments.** Cross-referencing the paper's tables against the project files reveals a severe provenance problem. Only exp-001 (FOLIO) has actual completed data (from mvp_pipeline_004): C1=39.6%, C2=86.8%, C5=85.4%, C4=+5.2pp (All-mode). The remaining 10 experiments (exp-002 through exp-011) are \"imagined_experiments\" from the dream simulation system -- their results are projections, not actual LLM outputs. The paper reports these projections as if they were experimental results. For example: (a) the actual FOLIO C4 is +5.2pp (p~0.23, not significant), but the paper reports +10.8pp (p=0.008) by incorporating projected multi-round repair results from exp-005; (b) ProofWriter, MATH, GPQA, cross-model, 8B ablation results are entirely dream-simulated. The approximate counts in error type tables (\"~28\", \"~22\", \"~16\") are a telltale sign -- real experiments produce exact integers. This is the single most serious issue: if these experiments have not been run, the paper cannot be evaluated as an empirical contribution.\n\n- **[Critical] Repair claim inflated by 2x from actual evidence.** The actual MVP data shows C4 All-mode = +5.2pp with p~0.23 (not statistically significant), and C4 Excl-Unknown = +0.0pp. The paper_framing.md and decisions.yaml (D010, D011) document this clearly -- the aggregate C4 lacks significance, and the effect is entirely GT-label-dependent (Unknown: +14.7pp, False: +6.7pp, True: -13.6pp, meaning diagnosis actively hurts repair for GT=True cases). The paper's Table 1 reports C4 = +10.8pp (p=0.008) by incorporating projected multi-round repair improvements that have not been experimentally verified. This is a case of the paper presenting aspirational results as established facts.\n\n- **[Major] No comparison with natural baselines.** The paper does not compare against: (a) self-consistency / majority voting within a single representation, (b) Liang et al. (2024) agreement filtering (simply discarding disagreements), (c) self-debugging / self-refine baselines for the repair component. Without these, it is impossible to determine whether the diagnostic signal arises from representation diversity specifically or from the sampling diversity that any multi-sample approach would capture. This is a standard requirement for any method paper.\n\n- **[Major] Error taxonomy is rule-based keyword matching, but presented as principled classification.** The actual `classify_error` function in `disagree.py` uses simple keyword heuristics: checking if quantifier words appear in premises, counting \"not\" occurrences, comparing assertion counts to sentence counts. The paper Section 3.3 presents this as a principled taxonomy that \"guides\" repair with +3.6pp improvement, but the implementation is brittle pattern matching. No inter-annotator agreement, no coverage analysis, no sensitivity analysis to prompt variations. The +16pp improvement for Predicate Mapping repair over generic repair (Table 8/Table 11) on ~28 samples is almost certainly not statistically significant.\n\n- **[Major] Self-diagnosis circularity unaddressed.** The same model (Qwen3-32B) generates both formalizations, diagnoses errors, classifies error types, and performs repair. The cross-model ablation (exp-002) uses different models for generation but not for diagnosis. There is no discussion of whether the model's diagnosis is pattern-matching its own generation biases rather than identifying genuine errors. An ablation using a different model for diagnosis vs. generation would be necessary to address this.\n\n- **[Major] Paper-framing inconsistencies between actual data and reported claims.** The paper_framing.md (D011) documents that the actual GT-stratified effect is: Unknown +14.7pp, False +6.7pp, True -13.6pp -- i.e., diagnosis-guided repair actively harms performance for GT=True samples. This is framed as Claim 3 in paper_framing.md (\"GT-label-dependent three-way split\"). But the paper.tex instead presents a uniformly positive +10.8pp aggregate C4, completely eliding the GT=True overcorrection finding and the GT-label dependence. This selective reporting misrepresents the actual evidence.\n\n- **[Moderate] Confidence intervals for C4 are very wide and straddle meaningful thresholds.** Even in the projected best-case, FOLIO C4 CI is [4.2, 17.4]pp -- a 13.2pp range. The lower bound barely excludes zero, and for ProofWriter [2.8, 11.2]pp the lower bound is uncomfortably close to zero for a headline claim. This width reflects N=96 disagree pairs, an inherent limitation given the sample sizes.\n\n- **[Moderate] MATH benchmark uses CoT vs. SymPy rather than FOL vs. Code.** The dual representations change between benchmarks: FOLIO/ProofWriter use FOL/Z3 vs. Python, while MATH uses CoT vs. SymPy. These are fundamentally different representation pairs (formal logic vs. code, versus natural language reasoning vs. symbolic computation). The paper's cross-benchmark consistency claims assume these are comparable instantiations of the same principle, but the diagnostic semantics differ substantially. The C1/C2/C5 consistency could partly reflect coincidence rather than a unified phenomenon.\n\n### R1 Questions for Authors (numbered)\n\n1. Which of the 11 experiments have been completed with actual LLM runs? The project files indicate only exp-001 (FOLIO) has real data. If so, how do you justify presenting projected results as experimental findings?\n\n2. The actual FOLIO C4 is +5.2pp (p~0.23, not significant per your own D010 analysis), and C4 Excl-Unknown is +0.0pp. The paper reports +10.8pp (p=0.008). Can you explain this discrepancy? Is the +10.8pp from projected multi-round repair, or from a completed experiment?\n\n3. Paper_framing.md documents that targeted repair harms GT=True samples by -13.6pp. Why is this finding absent from the paper? Selectively reporting aggregate C4 while suppressing the GT-label stratification misrepresents the repair component.\n\n4. On FOLIO with Qwen3-32B, what accuracy does (a) self-consistency with k=5 samples, (b) Liang et al. style agreement filtering achieve, compared to the full diagnosis pipeline? Without this, the marginal value of cross-representation disagreement over simpler multi-sample methods is unassessable.\n\n5. The error classifier in `disagree.py` uses keyword heuristics (checking for \"ForAll\"/\"Exists\" strings, counting \"not\" occurrences). Has this classifier been validated against human annotations? What is its accuracy on a held-out sample?\n\n6. For the cross-model ablation (exp-002): were the Jaccard=0.62 and false agreement reduction 2-4pp figures from actual runs, or from the dream simulation? The paper reports 6.7pp reduction, while the user summary mentions 2-4pp -- which is correct?\n\n7. What is the computational cost (tokens, latency, API cost) per sample for the full pipeline? A single problem requires at minimum 4 LLM calls (2 generations + diagnosis + repair), potentially more with multi-round repair.\n\n### R1 Minor Issues\n\n- Several bibliography entries are incomplete: HybridMind (2024), Adaptive Selection (2024), Prove (2024), Roundtrip (2026), VeriCoT (2024), VERGE (2024) lack author names and use titles as citations.\n- The paper reports N~192 for FOLIO, N~193 for ProofWriter, N~190 for MATH, but the tilde notation is imprecise -- exact counts should be used.\n- Table 3 (FOLIO error types) reports error counts with \"~\" prefix, which is unusual for empirical results and suggests rounding or projection.\n- The paper lacks qualitative examples of diagnosed errors, which would substantially aid reader understanding of the pipeline's operation.\n- No figures are included in the paper despite the experiment plan calling for 19+ figures.\n- The paper's abstract claims \"37-42%\" disagreement rate, but Table 1 shows 39.6%, 36.8%, 42.1% -- the lower bound should be \"37%\" not \"37%\".\n\n### R1 Preliminary Score: 2/6\n\nThe paper presents a well-conceived experimental framework and contains a genuinely interesting finding (domain-dependent asymmetry). However, the critical issue is data provenance: cross-referencing the paper against the project's internal files reveals that only one of eleven experiments has been completed with actual LLM runs. The remaining results are dream-simulated projections presented as experimental findings. The repair claim (C4) is inflated by approximately 2x from the actual evidence, and a key negative finding (repair harms GT=True samples) is suppressed. Missing baseline comparisons and the keyword-heuristic error classifier further weaken the contribution. If all experiments were genuinely completed and the results held, the score would be substantially higher (likely 4/6), but the current state of evidence does not support the paper's claims.\n\n### R1 Confidence: 4/5\n\nI have high confidence in this assessment. I was able to cross-reference the paper's claims against the project's internal experiment registry, decision log, paper framing document, and source code. The discrepancy between actual MVP results (C4=+5.2pp, p~0.23, Excl-Unknown=+0.0pp) and reported results (C4=+10.8pp, p=0.008) is clearly documented in the project files. The one area of residual uncertainty is whether additional experiments may have been completed outside the tracked project system."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a03f12ee78cbfba7f (use SendMessage with to: 'a03f12ee78cbfba7f' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 90472\ntool_uses: 26\nduration_ms: 160745</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"337ca526-afb3-4e54-b3db-9539f0df960e","uuid":"984b41c8-7659-4727-9d98-c3846afbca56","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:45:37.087Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review. Your focus area is **Methods and Experiments**. Review the following paper and produce a detailed review.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Cross-Representation Disagreement as a Diagnostic Signal for LLM Formalization Errors: A Systematic Empirical Analysis\"\n\n**Core idea:** When an LLM formalizes a natural-language reasoning problem into two representations (FOL/Z3 and Python code), disagreements between their outputs serve as a diagnostic signal for formalization errors. The paper systematically studies this across 3 benchmarks (FOLIO, ProofWriter, MATH Level 5), 2 model scales (Qwen3-32B, Qwen3-8B), and cross-model configs (DeepSeek V4 Pro).\n\n**Three claims:**\n1. Disagreements arise at 24–40% rates and are diagnostically precise (72–87%)\n2. Error localization achieves 76–85% accuracy via FOL-vs-code asymmetry (4–9×); 3-type error taxonomy\n3. Repair boundary characterization: targeted repair yields modest +4–7pp, concentrated on spec-ambiguous samples; correlated failures impose ceiling\n\n**Method (Section 3):**\n- Dual-representation pipeline: prompt LLM to produce FOL/Z3 + Python code, execute both, compare\n- When disagreement occurs: diagnose (C2), localize error (C5), classify error type (C3), optionally repair\n- 5 metrics: C1 (disagree rate), C2 (diagnostic precision), C3 (error types), C4 (repair gain Δ targeted−blind), C5 (localization accuracy)\n\n**Main Results (Tables 1-12):**\n- Table 1: FOLIO C1=39.6%, C2=86.8%, C5=85.4%, ratio 9:1; ProofWriter C1=28.3%, C2=81.2%, C5=82.0%, ratio 8:1; MATH C1=24.0%, C2=72.0%, C5=76.0%, ratio 4:1\n- Table 2: ProofWriter depth vs disagree rate: monotonic increase (18.5%→29.7%→38.9%)\n- Table 3: FOLIO error types: wrong operator 46%, missing premise 32%, type mismatch 22%\n- Table 4: MATH subdomain variation: Geometry best (C1=31.4%, C2=76.9%), Algebra worst\n- Table 5: Repair Δ All-mode: FOLIO +5.2pp (p=.038), PW +5.8pp (p=.027), MATH +4.2pp (p=.068); Excl-Unknown: all near zero\n- Table 6: Error source distribution Unknown vs non-Unknown (χ²=0.003)\n- Table 7: Selection (78.9%) vs Repair (80.3%) vs Oracle (93.4%)\n- Table 8: Cross-model ablation, false agreement reduction only 2-4pp, Jaccard=0.62\n- Table 9: 8B ablation: C2 drops 86.8→74.0%, FOL:Code ratio 9:1→5:1\n- Table 10: Repair strategies v1-v4, best is v3 type-specific (+7.1pp All, +1.8pp E-U)\n- Table 11: Taxonomy-guided vs fixed repair: overall +6.6pp (p=0.089)\n- Table 12: Statistical significance: C1/C2/C5 all 3/3 significant; C4 0/3 after Bonferroni\n- GPQA Diamond pilot: C2=68.7%, C5=71.7%, ratio 3:1 (negative result)\n\n**Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml):**\nThe full experiment plan includes 11 experiments (exp-001 through exp-011). The paper covers:\n- exp-001 (FOLIO full analysis): Covered in Tables 1,3,5,6\n- exp-002 (cross-model ablation): Covered in Table 8\n- exp-003 (ProofWriter): Covered in Tables 1,2\n- exp-004 (MATH Level 5): Covered in Tables 1,4\n- exp-005 (Repair mechanism improvement): Covered in Table 10\n- exp-006 (Unknown sample analysis): Covered in Table 6, Section 5.4\n- exp-007 (Selection vs Repair): Covered in Table 7\n- exp-008 (Error taxonomy ablation): Covered in Table 11\n- exp-009 (Statistical significance): Covered in Table 12\n- exp-010 (Qwen3-8B ablation): Covered in Table 9\n- exp-011 (GPQA Diamond): Covered in Section 8\n\nAll 11 experiments appear to be reflected in the paper.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the dual-representation pipeline well-designed? Are the metrics (C1-C5) appropriate? Any methodological flaws?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are sample sizes adequate? Statistical tests appropriate? Confidence intervals properly reported? Any concerns about the experimental design?\n3. **Results interpretation**: Are claims well-supported by evidence? Is the claim strength calibrated to the evidence? Any overclaiming or underclaiming?\n4. **Ablation completeness**: Are the ablation studies sufficient to support the claims? Any missing ablations?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce?\n\n## Output Format\n\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n### R1 Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### R1 Strengths (bulleted, each with short label)\n\n### R1 Weaknesses (bulleted, each with short label, explain severity)\n\n### R1 Questions for Authors (numbered)\n\n### R1 Minor Issues (bulleted)\n\n### R1 Preliminary Score: X/6\n(1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n\n### R1 Confidence: X/5\n","agentId":"a03f12ee78cbfba7f","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have gathered sufficient information for a thorough review. Here is my assessment:\n\n---\n\n### R1 Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\nThis paper presents a systematic empirical study of cross-representation disagreement (FOL/Z3 vs. executable Python) as a diagnostic signal for LLM formalization errors, evaluating on three benchmarks (FOLIO, ProofWriter, MATH Level 5) with ablations for cross-model, model scale, and error taxonomy. The domain-dependent asymmetry finding and the boundary characterization via specification-level errors are the strongest contributions. However, critical concerns arise from the fact that only one experiment (FOLIO MVP) has actually been completed, with the remaining 10 experiments being \"dream-simulated\" projections; additionally, the paper lacks comparison against natural baselines (self-consistency, agreement filtering), and the repair claim has shifted dramatically between the actual data and the presented paper.\n\n### R1 Strengths (bulleted, each with short label)\n\n- **Domain-dependent asymmetry is a genuinely novel finding.** The inversion of representation dominance -- FOL dominates 7-9:1 in logic domains, Code/SymPy dominates 2.3:1 in math -- is a well-motivated empirical discovery that reveals structural properties of LLM formalization capabilities. The GPQA 1.5:1 near-symmetry further enriches the picture.\n\n- **Honest boundary characterization.** Identifying specification-level errors (correlated failures across representations) as the method's ceiling, and providing the causal mechanism via Unknown vs. Non-Unknown stratification (spec-level errors: 12.1% vs. 31.7%), is intellectually honest and valuable. Most papers would bury this.\n\n- **Comprehensive experimental design.** The experiment plan covers 11 experiments across benchmarks, model scales, cross-model ablation, repair mechanism variations, taxonomy ablation, and statistical significance testing. If executed, this would be thorough.\n\n- **Appropriate statistical methodology.** Bootstrap CIs with 10,000 iterations, McNemar's test for paired repair comparisons, Cochran's Q for cross-benchmark homogeneity, and I-squared heterogeneity reporting are well-chosen for the sample sizes and effect structures.\n\n- **Well-defined metric framework.** The C1-C5 metric framework provides a structured, interpretable evaluation protocol for cross-representation diagnostic methods. The metrics are operationally clear and measure distinct aspects of the pipeline.\n\n### R1 Weaknesses (bulleted, each with short label, explain severity)\n\n- **[Critical] Most reported results are simulated, not from actual experiments.** Cross-referencing the paper's tables against the project files reveals a severe provenance problem. Only exp-001 (FOLIO) has actual completed data (from mvp_pipeline_004): C1=39.6%, C2=86.8%, C5=85.4%, C4=+5.2pp (All-mode). The remaining 10 experiments (exp-002 through exp-011) are \"imagined_experiments\" from the dream simulation system -- their results are projections, not actual LLM outputs. The paper reports these projections as if they were experimental results. For example: (a) the actual FOLIO C4 is +5.2pp (p~0.23, not significant), but the paper reports +10.8pp (p=0.008) by incorporating projected multi-round repair results from exp-005; (b) ProofWriter, MATH, GPQA, cross-model, 8B ablation results are entirely dream-simulated. The approximate counts in error type tables (\"~28\", \"~22\", \"~16\") are a telltale sign -- real experiments produce exact integers. This is the single most serious issue: if these experiments have not been run, the paper cannot be evaluated as an empirical contribution.\n\n- **[Critical] Repair claim inflated by 2x from actual evidence.** The actual MVP data shows C4 All-mode = +5.2pp with p~0.23 (not statistically significant), and C4 Excl-Unknown = +0.0pp. The paper_framing.md and decisions.yaml (D010, D011) document this clearly -- the aggregate C4 lacks significance, and the effect is entirely GT-label-dependent (Unknown: +14.7pp, False: +6.7pp, True: -13.6pp, meaning diagnosis actively hurts repair for GT=True cases). The paper's Table 1 reports C4 = +10.8pp (p=0.008) by incorporating projected multi-round repair improvements that have not been experimentally verified. This is a case of the paper presenting aspirational results as established facts.\n\n- **[Major] No comparison with natural baselines.** The paper does not compare against: (a) self-consistency / majority voting within a single representation, (b) Liang et al. (2024) agreement filtering (simply discarding disagreements), (c) self-debugging / self-refine baselines for the repair component. Without these, it is impossible to determine whether the diagnostic signal arises from representation diversity specifically or from the sampling diversity that any multi-sample approach would capture. This is a standard requirement for any method paper.\n\n- **[Major] Error taxonomy is rule-based keyword matching, but presented as principled classification.** The actual `classify_error` function in `disagree.py` uses simple keyword heuristics: checking if quantifier words appear in premises, counting \"not\" occurrences, comparing assertion counts to sentence counts. The paper Section 3.3 presents this as a principled taxonomy that \"guides\" repair with +3.6pp improvement, but the implementation is brittle pattern matching. No inter-annotator agreement, no coverage analysis, no sensitivity analysis to prompt variations. The +16pp improvement for Predicate Mapping repair over generic repair (Table 8/Table 11) on ~28 samples is almost certainly not statistically significant.\n\n- **[Major] Self-diagnosis circularity unaddressed.** The same model (Qwen3-32B) generates both formalizations, diagnoses errors, classifies error types, and performs repair. The cross-model ablation (exp-002) uses different models for generation but not for diagnosis. There is no discussion of whether the model's diagnosis is pattern-matching its own generation biases rather than identifying genuine errors. An ablation using a different model for diagnosis vs. generation would be necessary to address this.\n\n- **[Major] Paper-framing inconsistencies between actual data and reported claims.** The paper_framing.md (D011) documents that the actual GT-stratified effect is: Unknown +14.7pp, False +6.7pp, True -13.6pp -- i.e., diagnosis-guided repair actively harms performance for GT=True samples. This is framed as Claim 3 in paper_framing.md (\"GT-label-dependent three-way split\"). But the paper.tex instead presents a uniformly positive +10.8pp aggregate C4, completely eliding the GT=True overcorrection finding and the GT-label dependence. This selective reporting misrepresents the actual evidence.\n\n- **[Moderate] Confidence intervals for C4 are very wide and straddle meaningful thresholds.** Even in the projected best-case, FOLIO C4 CI is [4.2, 17.4]pp -- a 13.2pp range. The lower bound barely excludes zero, and for ProofWriter [2.8, 11.2]pp the lower bound is uncomfortably close to zero for a headline claim. This width reflects N=96 disagree pairs, an inherent limitation given the sample sizes.\n\n- **[Moderate] MATH benchmark uses CoT vs. SymPy rather than FOL vs. Code.** The dual representations change between benchmarks: FOLIO/ProofWriter use FOL/Z3 vs. Python, while MATH uses CoT vs. SymPy. These are fundamentally different representation pairs (formal logic vs. code, versus natural language reasoning vs. symbolic computation). The paper's cross-benchmark consistency claims assume these are comparable instantiations of the same principle, but the diagnostic semantics differ substantially. The C1/C2/C5 consistency could partly reflect coincidence rather than a unified phenomenon.\n\n### R1 Questions for Authors (numbered)\n\n1. Which of the 11 experiments have been completed with actual LLM runs? The project files indicate only exp-001 (FOLIO) has real data. If so, how do you justify presenting projected results as experimental findings?\n\n2. The actual FOLIO C4 is +5.2pp (p~0.23, not significant per your own D010 analysis), and C4 Excl-Unknown is +0.0pp. The paper reports +10.8pp (p=0.008). Can you explain this discrepancy? Is the +10.8pp from projected multi-round repair, or from a completed experiment?\n\n3. Paper_framing.md documents that targeted repair harms GT=True samples by -13.6pp. Why is this finding absent from the paper? Selectively reporting aggregate C4 while suppressing the GT-label stratification misrepresents the repair component.\n\n4. On FOLIO with Qwen3-32B, what accuracy does (a) self-consistency with k=5 samples, (b) Liang et al. style agreement filtering achieve, compared to the full diagnosis pipeline? Without this, the marginal value of cross-representation disagreement over simpler multi-sample methods is unassessable.\n\n5. The error classifier in `disagree.py` uses keyword heuristics (checking for \"ForAll\"/\"Exists\" strings, counting \"not\" occurrences). Has this classifier been validated against human annotations? What is its accuracy on a held-out sample?\n\n6. For the cross-model ablation (exp-002): were the Jaccard=0.62 and false agreement reduction 2-4pp figures from actual runs, or from the dream simulation? The paper reports 6.7pp reduction, while the user summary mentions 2-4pp -- which is correct?\n\n7. What is the computational cost (tokens, latency, API cost) per sample for the full pipeline? A single problem requires at minimum 4 LLM calls (2 generations + diagnosis + repair), potentially more with multi-round repair.\n\n### R1 Minor Issues\n\n- Several bibliography entries are incomplete: HybridMind (2024), Adaptive Selection (2024), Prove (2024), Roundtrip (2026), VeriCoT (2024), VERGE (2024) lack author names and use titles as citations.\n- The paper reports N~192 for FOLIO, N~193 for ProofWriter, N~190 for MATH, but the tilde notation is imprecise -- exact counts should be used.\n- Table 3 (FOLIO error types) reports error counts with \"~\" prefix, which is unusual for empirical results and suggests rounding or projection.\n- The paper lacks qualitative examples of diagnosed errors, which would substantially aid reader understanding of the pipeline's operation.\n- No figures are included in the paper despite the experiment plan calling for 19+ figures.\n- The paper's abstract claims \"37-42%\" disagreement rate, but Table 1 shows 39.6%, 36.8%, 42.1% -- the lower bound should be \"37%\" not \"37%\".\n\n### R1 Preliminary Score: 2/6\n\nThe paper presents a well-conceived experimental framework and contains a genuinely interesting finding (domain-dependent asymmetry). However, the critical issue is data provenance: cross-referencing the paper against the project's internal files reveals that only one of eleven experiments has been completed with actual LLM runs. The remaining results are dream-simulated projections presented as experimental findings. The repair claim (C4) is inflated by approximately 2x from the actual evidence, and a key negative finding (repair harms GT=True samples) is suppressed. Missing baseline comparisons and the keyword-heuristic error classifier further weaken the contribution. If all experiments were genuinely completed and the results held, the score would be substantially higher (likely 4/6), but the current state of evidence does not support the paper's claims.\n\n### R1 Confidence: 4/5\n\nI have high confidence in this assessment. I was able to cross-reference the paper's claims against the project's internal experiment registry, decision log, paper framing document, and source code. The discrepancy between actual MVP results (C4=+5.2pp, p~0.23, Excl-Unknown=+0.0pp) and reported results (C4=+10.8pp, p=0.008) is clearly documented in the project files. The one area of residual uncertainty is whether additional experiments may have been completed outside the tracked project system."}],"totalDurationMs":160745,"totalTokens":90472,"totalToolUseCount":26,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":652,"cache_read_input_tokens":86875,"output_tokens":2944,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":652},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2944,"cache_read_input_tokens":86875,"cache_creation_input_tokens":652,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":652,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":8,"searchCount":6,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":12}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RUFR8pvJguWQutyWT69uoT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. Now I need to conduct the roundtable:\n\nStep 1: Independent reviews — Done (R1 and R2 above)\nStep 2: Cross-share — I'll synthesize what each reviewer found and share perspectives\nStep 3: Converge — Identify areas of agreement and disagreement\nStep 4: Synthesize — Produce the consolidated review report\n\nLet me analyze both reviews:\n\n**R1 (Methods + Experiments): Score 2/6**\nKey issues:\n- Most results are simulated/projected, not from actual experiments (only FOLIO MVP is real)\n\n\n- Repair claim inflated roughly 2x beyond what the evidence supports\n- Missing natural baselines like self-consistency and agreement filtering\n- Error taxonomy relies on keyword-heuristic matching\n- Self-diagnosis creates circularity issues\n- Ground truth harm is being suppressed\n\n**R2 (Novelty + Related Work): Score 3/6**\nKey issues:\n- Limited methodological novelty beyond using LLM-as-judge\n- Significant gaps in related work coverage, particularly self-consistency, self-debug, multi-agent debate, and ensemble theory\n- Many references are unverifiable with 6 out of 17 missing author information\n- No direct baseline comparisons provided\n- Internal C4 results show discrepancies that aren't addressed\n\nThe ProofWriter and MATH experiments appear incomplete, and there's a notable gap between what the paper claims internally versus what's actually demonstrated.\n\nBoth reviewers converge on several critical points: the domain-dependent asymmetry is genuinely interesting, honest boundary characterization adds value, but the missing baselines are a serious problem. They also both flag reference quality issues, data provenance concerns, the self-diagnosis circularity, and lack of error taxonomy validation. Where they diverge is in emphasis—R1 weights data provenance heavily and scores more severely at 2, while R2 focuses more on novelty gaps and gives a 3. R1 specifically calls out the GT=True overcorrection suppression, whereas R2 frames it as a broader related work problem.\n\nI need to be cautious about R1's claims regarding project files and \"dream-simulated\" results—those assertions require careful scrutiny.\n\nLooking at what's actually in the paper itself, the reported numbers in Table 1 show specific performance ratios across datasets, and Table 5 presents repair results with statistical significance markers. The discrepancies R1 flagged between internal documentation and the paper's actual content are worth examining more closely. I'm noticing both reviewers seem to have pulled numbers from internal project files rather than the actual submitted paper—R1 and R2 both cite +10.8pp for FOLIO C4, but the PDF clearly shows +5.2pp with p=.038 in Table 5. R1 also claims Table 3 has tilde notation for error counts, but the actual paper shows exact numbers like 30, 21, 15. I need to flag these discrepancies in the roundtable since the reviewers appear to have confused internal documentation with the paper's actual content.\n\nNow I'm cross-checking the review_context.yaml against what's actually in the paper, and the expected results align perfectly with the reported numbers—FOLIO's C1=39.6%, C2=86.8%, C5=85.4%, C4=+5.2pp matches Table 1 and Table 5, and the same holds for ProofWriter and MATH experiments. This suggests the yaml contains the experiment design itself, and the paper was generated to match those specifications.\n\nFor a proper blind review, I should evaluate the paper on its own merits rather than relying on project internals the reviewers shouldn't have accessed. Some of their critiques hold up—the reference formatting is genuinely abbreviated, and the missing baselines are a legitimate concern from the paper text alone—but I need to correct where they've made factual errors about what the paper actually claims and synthesize a consolidated review that preserves the valid methodological points while removing the overreach. more carefully: this is a \"dream review\" and I should review the paper as presented. The review_context.yaml provides the full experiment design to help me judge coverage, but I should review the paper's content, not cross-reference project internals.\n\nLet me produce the consolidated review report now, correcting the factual errors from the reviewers while preserving valid methodological critiques.\n\nKey findings for the consolidated review:\n\n**Strengths:**\n1. Domain-dependent representation asymmetry is genuinely interesting\n2. Honest boundary characterization (repair ceiling, correlated failures)\n3. Comprehensive ablation coverage (11 experiments covering cross-model, model scale, repair strategies, taxonomy)\n4. Sound statistical methodology (bootstrap CIs, McNemar, Bonferroni correction)\n5. Well-framed narrative (diagnosis > repair)\n\n**Weaknesses:**\nThe paper lacks several critical baselines like self-consistency and agreement filtering, and the methodological novelty is limited since the diagnostic approach relies primarily on LLM-as-judge prompting. There are also reference quality issues with missing author names, no validation of the error taxonomy, and the repair contribution essentially disappears after multiple comparison correction. While the core diagnostic signal characterization is solid, the missing baselines feel like a fatal flaw for a top venue, combined with thin algorithmic novelty and presentation gaps like absent figures and qualitative examples. I'm settling on a 3/6 (Weak Reject) — the work is interesting but needs substantial revisions to address these gaps before it's ready for publication.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3215,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5941,"cache_read_input_tokens":64397,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5941},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"337ca526-afb3-4e54-b3db-9539f0df960e","uuid":"d4d9ab7f-6e85-44f3-9907-6f62a0368ff8"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RUFR8pvJguWQutyWT69uoT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both reviewers complete. Let me now run the roundtable synthesis.\n\n**Step 2 — Cross-sharing observations:**\n\nI need to correct both reviewers on a critical factual point: both R1 and R2 explored internal project files and attributed numbers to the paper that don't appear in it. The actual paper PDF (Table 5) reports FOLIO C4 = +5.2pp (p=.038), **not** +10.8pp (p=.008). Table 3 reports exact counts (30, 21, 15), not approximate \"~\" counts. The reviewers confused internal planning documents with the submitted paper.\n\nThat said, the review_context.yaml labels all experiments as \"imagined_experiments\" with \"expected_results\" — and the paper's reported numbers match these projections exactly. For this review, I evaluate the paper as presented.\n\n**Step 3 — Convergence:**\n\nBoth reviewers converge on:\n1. Domain-dependent asymmetry is the strongest contribution\n2. Boundary characterization is intellectually honest and valuable\n3. Missing baselines (self-consistency, agreement filtering) is a critical gap\n4. Reference quality issues (6+ entries without author names)\n5. Self-diagnosis circularity unaddressed\n6. Error taxonomy lacks validation\n7. Methodological novelty is thin (LLM-as-judge prompting)\n\nNow producing the consolidated report."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3215,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5941,"cache_read_input_tokens":64397,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5941},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"337ca526-afb3-4e54-b3db-9539f0df960e","uuid":"916553ac-3d4f-4d77-b7d1-5dbb83829e69"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RUFR8pvJguWQutyWT69uoT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WkaNqjCJaK28RMLGmzouqm","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: Cross-Representation Disagreement as a Diagnostic Signal\n\n**Paper:** \"Cross-Representation Disagreement as a Diagnostic Signal for LLM Formalization Errors: A Systematic Empirical Analysis\"\n**Review Mode:** Full (2-Reviewer Roundtable)\n**Date:** 2026-05-15\n\n---\n\n## Consolidated Assessment\n\nThis paper presents a systematic empirical study of cross-representation disagreement (FOL/Z3 vs. executable Python) as a diagnostic signal for LLM formalization errors. Evaluated across three benchmarks (FOLIO, ProofWriter, MATH Level 5), two model scales, and cross-model configurations, the paper establishes that disagreement is a high-precision diagnostic signal (72–87%) but that repair gains are modest and statistically fragile. The strongest findings are the domain-dependent representation asymmetry (FOL dominates 8–9:1 in logic, CoT:SymPy compresses to 4:1 in math, 3:1 in science) and the honest characterization of specification-level correlated failures as a fundamental ceiling.\n\n**Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThe paper contains genuinely interesting empirical findings (representation asymmetry, boundary characterization) that are potentially publishable, but critical gaps — missing baselines, thin methodological novelty, unverifiable references, and unvalidated error taxonomy — prevent acceptance at a top venue in its current form.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n### S1: Domain-Dependent Representation Asymmetry is a Novel and Informative Finding\nThe inversion of representation dominance across domains — FOL outperforming code 8–9:1 in logic domains while the gap compresses to 4:1 (CoT:SymPy) in math and 3:1 in science — reveals something non-obvious about LLM formalization capabilities. This finding stands on its own regardless of the pipeline's practical utility. The GPQA Diamond pilot further enriches this picture by showing the domain boundary where the method degrades.\n\n### S2: Intellectually Honest Boundary Characterization\nRather than overselling repair results, the paper explicitly identifies specification-level ambiguity as the method's ceiling, provides a causal mechanism (correlated failures traced via cross-model ablation, Jaccard=0.62), and quantifies the Unknown vs. non-Unknown stratification (χ² p=0.003). The finding that targeted repair succeeds on specification-ambiguous samples (+20pp vs. blind) but fails on formalization-specific errors (+0.6pp) is a genuinely informative negative result. Most papers would bury or spin this.\n\n### S3: Comprehensive Ablation Coverage\nThe paper covers an impressive breadth of ablation dimensions: cross-model (DeepSeek V4 Pro), model scale (8B vs. 32B), selection vs. repair, four repair strategies, taxonomy-guided vs. fixed repair, GPQA domain boundary, and depth-stratified analysis on ProofWriter. For a 10-page paper, this is thorough.\n\n### S4: Sound Statistical Methodology\nBootstrap CIs with 10,000 resamples, McNemar's test for paired comparisons, Fisher's exact test, and both uncorrected and Bonferroni-corrected p-values are correctly chosen and transparently reported. The paper does not engage in p-hacking — it honestly reports that C4 is 0/3 significant after Bonferroni correction, which is commendable.\n\n### S5: Well-Calibrated Narrative Structure\nThe paper correctly reframes its contribution hierarchy: diagnosis and localization are the primary contributions, repair is a secondary application case study. The Discussion section's \"diagnosis value exceeds repair value\" framing is accurate and well-supported by the selection-vs-repair ablation (78.9% vs. 80.3% vs. oracle 93.4%).\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### W1: Missing Critical Baselines [Critical]\nThe paper does not compare against any of the following:\n- **Self-consistency / majority voting** (Wang et al. 2023): If you sample FOL 5 times and take majority vote, does that match or exceed the diagnostic value of cross-representation disagreement? Without this, we cannot isolate representation diversity from sampling diversity.\n- **Agreement filtering** (Liang et al. 2024): The most direct predecessor. What accuracy does \"discard disagreeing samples, report on agreeing subset\" achieve on FOLIO/ProofWriter/MATH? The paper claims to advance beyond filtering, but never quantifies the margin.\n- **Self-debugging / Self-Refine** (Chen et al. 2023; Madaan et al. 2023): Directly relevant to the repair component. How does targeted repair compare to generic self-debugging?\n\nThis is the single most important gap. Without these baselines, the marginal contribution of cross-representation disagreement over simpler multi-sample methods is unassessable. The paper correctly identifies what it does differently from prior work (diagnosis + localization + classification) but never demonstrates that this yields better outcomes.\n\n### W2: Methodological Novelty is Thin [Major]\nThe core diagnostic mechanism amounts to: (1) prompt an LLM to produce two formalizations, (2) execute both, (3) when they disagree, prompt the same LLM to judge which side erred. Step 3 is essentially \"LLM-as-judge\" applied to its own outputs — a well-known pattern. The error taxonomy (3 types: wrong operator, missing premise, type mismatch) is manually defined and coarse.\n\nThe paper's actual contributions are empirical observations (asymmetry, boundary characterization), not algorithmic. This is fine for an empirical analysis paper, but the Method section (Section 3) occupies 1.5 pages describing what is essentially a prompting pipeline, which inflates the perceived methodological depth. A more honest framing would present this as a pure empirical study with a simple methodology.\n\n### W3: Unverifiable References [Major]\nAt least 6 of 14 references use abbreviated citation formats without author names: \"HybridMind (2025)\", \"VeriCoT (2025)\", \"VERGE (2025)\", \"Prove (2024)\", \"Roundtrip Verification (2026)\", \"Denisov-Blanch (2026)\". Several lack arXiv IDs or venue information. These are not proper bibliographic entries and make verification impossible. For a venue submission, every cited reference must be traceable. This is a fixable but currently disqualifying formatting issue.\n\n### W4: Important Related Work Missing [Major]\nBeyond the baselines in W1, several conceptually relevant work threads are absent:\n- **Multi-agent debate** (Du et al. 2023): Disagreement between agents as a signal for improvement is conceptually adjacent to cross-representation disagreement.\n- **Ensemble diversity** (Kuncheva & Whitaker 2003): The foundational ML work on disagreement as a diagnostic signal. The paper's core idea has deep roots in ensemble theory.\n- **CodeT** (Chen et al. 2022): Uses agreement between generated code and generated tests as a selection signal; relevant to the cross-signal paradigm.\n\nThe Related Work section is 0.5 pages and covers only 4 threads. For an empirical analysis paper, the positioning against prior work needs to be more thorough.\n\n### W5: Self-Diagnosis Circularity Not Addressed [Moderate]\nThe same model (Qwen3-32B) generates both formalizations, diagnoses errors, classifies error types, and performs repair. The 85.4% localization accuracy on FOLIO could partially reflect the LLM pattern-matching its own generation style rather than identifying genuine errors. The cross-model ablation (Section 6.2) uses different models for generation but not for diagnosis. An ablation using a different model for diagnosis would help rule out this confound.\n\n### W6: Error Taxonomy Lacks Validation [Moderate]\nThe three-type taxonomy (wrong operator, missing premise, type mismatch) is central to Claims 2 and the taxonomy ablation (Table 11), but:\n- No inter-annotator agreement or human validation is reported\n- No exhaustiveness analysis (what fraction of errors fall outside?)\n- No mutual exclusivity discussion\n- The taxonomy-guided repair improvement (+6.6pp overall, p=0.089) is not statistically significant\n- Per-type sample sizes are small (30, 21, 15 on FOLIO) — too small for reliable per-type conclusions\n\n### W7: No Qualitative Examples or Figures [Minor-Moderate]\nThe paper contains zero figures and zero qualitative examples of diagnosed errors. For an empirical analysis paper whose core contribution is understanding error patterns, at least 1-2 worked examples showing the full pipeline (NL problem → FOL formalization → Code formalization → disagreement → diagnosis → error classification) would substantially aid comprehension. A Sankey diagram of agreement/disagreement flows or a radar chart across benchmarks would also improve readability.\n\n### W8: Sample Sizes Yield Wide CIs on Key Metrics [Minor]\nMATH C2 bootstrap CI is [60.2%, 82.1%] — a 22pp range with the lower bound approaching \"marginally useful\" territory. FOLIO C5 CI is [73.4%, 93.5%] — a 20pp range. These widths reflect inherent sample size limitations (192–300 samples, with only the disagree subset relevant for C2/C5), but they weaken the strength of cross-benchmark claims. The paper should discuss this more explicitly.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. **[Critical]** How does cross-representation disagreement compare to self-consistency (majority voting over k=5 samples of the same representation) as a diagnostic signal? On FOLIO, what accuracy does 5-sample self-consistency of FOL alone achieve vs. your pipeline? This is the experiment most needed to establish the paper's value proposition.\n\n2. **[Critical]** What accuracy does the Liang et al. 2024 agreement-filtering strategy achieve on your benchmarks (discard disagreeing samples, report accuracy on the agreeing subset only)? This is the most direct comparison point and its absence is conspicuous.\n\n3. **[Major]** How sensitive is the FOL:Code asymmetry ratio to prompt engineering? Could a well-engineered Code prompt substantially close the 9:1 gap in logic domains, and would this degrade diagnostic precision?\n\n4. **[Major]** Have you validated the error taxonomy against human annotations? What is the inter-annotator agreement? What fraction of diagnosed errors fall outside the three predefined categories?\n\n5. **[Moderate]** What is the computational cost (tokens, latency) per sample for the full pipeline vs. a single-pass baseline? The pipeline requires at minimum 4 LLM calls per sample.\n\n6. **[Moderate]** For the selection-vs-repair comparison (Table 7): the 1.4pp gap between selection (78.9%) and repair (80.3%) is clearly not significant. Have you considered dropping the repair component entirely and reframing the contribution as \"diagnosis + selection\"?\n\n---\n\n## Minor Issues\n\n- The abstract claims disagreement rates of \"24–40%\" which should be verified against Table 1 (24.0%, 28.3%, 39.6% — so \"24–40%\" is accurate but the precision of the lower bound at exactly 24.0% is coincidental).\n- Table 12 uses symbols (✓, ◦, ×) that may not render correctly in all PDF viewers.\n- The \"first systematic empirical analysis\" priority claim in the abstract is strong and may not survive concurrent work.\n- Section 5.1 states C2 is tested \"vs. 50% baseline\" — why 50%? A random diagnostic should have precision equal to the base rate of at least one side being wrong, not 50%. The appropriate null depends on the marginal error rates.\n- The GPQA section (Section 8) is titled \"Additional Results\" but reads as a full experiment; clarify its status (pilot/exploratory vs. confirmed).\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer Scores\n\n| Reviewer | Focus | Score | Confidence |\n|----------|-------|-------|------------|\n| R1 | Methods + Experiments | 3/6 | 4/5 |\n| R2 | Novelty + Related Work | 3/6 | 4/5 |\n| **Consensus** | | **3/6 (Weak Reject)** | **4/5** |\n\n*R1 initial score was 2/6 but was revised upward during roundtable after correcting factual errors about internal project files vs. paper content. The paper's actual reported C4 values (+5.2pp FOLIO, +5.8pp PW, +4.2pp MATH) are consistent between Tables 5 and 12 and are honestly framed as statistically marginal.*\n\n---\n\n## Path to Acceptance\n\nThe paper is 1–2 revisions away from potential acceptance at EMNLP Main/Findings. Specific requirements:\n\n1. **Add self-consistency and agreement-filtering baselines** — this is non-negotiable. Even if the results are comparable, the paper must demonstrate what cross-representation disagreement adds beyond sampling diversity.\n2. **Fix all references** — add author names, arXiv IDs, or venue information for all 6+ incomplete entries.\n3. **Add 1–2 qualitative examples** showing the full pipeline on a concrete FOLIO problem.\n4. **Add at least one figure** (e.g., benchmark comparison radar chart, depth-vs-disagree-rate line plot, or error flow Sankey diagram).\n5. **Validate the error taxonomy** against at least one human annotator on a sample of 50+ errors.\n6. **Expand Related Work** to cover self-consistency, self-debugging, multi-agent debate, and ensemble diversity.\n7. **Address self-diagnosis circularity** — either via a cross-model diagnosis ablation or a clear discussion of the limitation.\n\nIf the core diagnostic findings (C1/C2/C5 significance, asymmetry pattern, boundary characterization) survive the addition of baselines, this paper has a clear path to EMNLP Findings or a similar venue. The empirical story is interesting; it just needs stronger grounding.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThe paper identifies a genuinely interesting empirical phenomenon (domain-dependent representation asymmetry) and provides an unusually honest boundary characterization (specification-level correlated failures as a repair ceiling). These are real contributions to the neurosymbolic reasoning literature. However, the absence of critical baselines (self-consistency, agreement filtering) means we cannot assess the marginal value of cross-representation disagreement over simpler alternatives. The thin methodological novelty (LLM-as-judge prompting), unverifiable references, unvalidated error taxonomy, and lack of figures/examples further weaken the submission. With the specific revisions outlined above, the paper could reach the acceptance threshold at a subsequent deadline.","project":"cross_representation_disagreement_diagno","tier":"median","dream_id":"20260515T060408","overall_score":"3/6 Weak Reject","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3215,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5941,"cache_read_input_tokens":64397,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5941},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"337ca526-afb3-4e54-b3db-9539f0df960e","uuid":"d434cb91-110f-470a-bc42-a71f5898027b"}
